-- title:
--   Fibonacci Dijkstra: reusable correctness and running time
-- statement:
--   This result is part of the [Algo2Lean](https://www.algo2lean.com/) formalization series for *Introduction to Algorithms* (CLRS), fourth edition.
--
--   For a represented directed graph with V vertices, E adjacency entries, nonnegative integer weights and source 0 ≤ s < V, the compiled Fibonacci Dijkstra Call terminates and returns a fresh valid distance sequence. It contains shortest distances, with -1 for unreachable vertices. The graph and every pre-existing caller sequence, object and typed collection are preserved.
--
--   For the sum T of all seven heap clocks and six client clocks, measured as increments during this call,
--
--   $$T\leq 23(E+1)+\bigl(12(2\lfloor\log_2 V\rfloor+2)+3\bigr)V.$$
--
--   The finite bound has scale O(E+V log V). It counts the explicit source ticks, excluding graph construction and uninstrumented machine operations. This existential Call contract provides one final heap satisfying every guarantee together. Clients reuse the returned graph, distances, frame and cost without reproving either Dijkstra or Fibonacci heaps. CLRS fourth edition: Chapter 22, §22.3, Theorem 22.6; heap analysis uses the online Fibonacci supplement.
--
--   The `.algo` code closely follows the textbook pseudocode: initialization,
--   minimum extraction, and adjacency relaxation appear as explicit source loops
--   and operations. Representation choices are made explicit: `-1` denotes
--   infinity, flags record settled vertices, and only discovered vertices enter
--   the queue. The routine returns distances without a predecessor tree. Thus it
--   preserves the textbook's shortest-distance algorithm for nonnegative weights,
--   while differing from its all-vertices initial queue and literal notation.
--
--   ## Algorithm source
--
--   Project import root: `examples`. Entry file: `dijkstra/dijkstra_fibonacci.algo`.
